  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 101 PM CST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nContinuing towards midweek, Tuesday and Wednesday will be pretty\nsimilar to today, with afternoon highs in the 40s and overnight\nlows in the 20s and upper teens. Those running the Turkey Trot \nThursday morning will need to bundle up with those chilly\ntemperatures.\n\nSnow for Some on Thanksgiving?: \n\nAs we get closer to the Thanksgiving Holiday, things get much \nmore uncertain regarding precipitation chances, as models have \nbeen picking up on two upper level systems that are progged to \ninfluence the region Wednesday night through Thursday morning. \nAn upper level trough currently over the western CONUS will \ncontinue pushing east towards the region before becoming a \nclosed low, as a secondary upper level closed low pivots \nsoutheast towards the region. Depending on what model you look \nat, these two low pressure systems look to either phase together\nor \"wobble\" around each other, increasing the moisture content \nin the already cold airmass. Global models have been trending \nthe system further south and west, which has introduced snow \nchances in our northeast CWA, primarily along and northeast of \nHighway 63. While the majority of the snowfall continues to be \nnortheast of the area, here are the current LREF 24-hour \nsnowfall probabilities:\n\n> 0.1\": 35-55% \n> 0.5\": 25-45% \n> 1.0\": 20-40% \n> 1.5\": 15-30% \n\nwith the higher probabilities in those ranges occurring\nnortheast of Highway 63. With there still being uncertainties \nbetween models regarding the positioning, progression, and \nplacement of these systems, the snowfall forecast will likely \nchange over the next couple days. Regardless, with it being a \nHoliday and snowfall expected somewhere in the Mid-Mississippi \nand Ohio Valleys, those traveling for the holidays will need to\nremain cautious and take the necessary actions to remain safe \nif any impacts do occur. \n\nOnce these lows move out of the area, a warmer airmass will\nbegin to raise temperatures closer to normal to end the week,\nwith highs climbing back into the 50s on Friday, and into the\nupper 50s/low 60s through the weekend. Ensemble members continue\nto show an upper level shortwave moving towards the region\ntowards the end of the weekend, however there's still large\ninconsistencies regarding timing and placement. Any\nprecipitation chances remain <15% at this time, but with this\nbeing towards the end of the forecast period, we'll need to continue\nto monitor.",
